\subsection{Housing projections}\label{sec:r-homes}

Projections and observations differ in kind: build-out is an end state, while the observed count is the gross number
of homes added in a two- or four-year window (Table~\ref{tab:homes}).
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Ranking districts.} A five-year trend ranks districts better than build-out and likelihood $\times$
build-out in @@of_trend@@ cities: Spearman @@trend_rho_lo@@ to @@trend_rho_hi@@, against @@lk_rho_lo@@ to @@lk_rho_hi@@.
Likelihood $\times$ empirical yield (below) reaches @@ly_rho_lo@@ to @@ly_rho_hi@@, as well as the trend or better
in @@of_yield_trend@@.
\item \textbf{Build-out.} A proxy of zoning and plan build-out (each lot at the density that lots of its zoned or
planned class reach today, not legal capacity) exceeds the homes added in the window @@bo_lo@@- to @@bo_hi@@-fold.
\item \textbf{Totals.} Likelihood weighting brings totals to @@lk_lo@@--@@lk_hi@@ times the observed. Using the homes
that developing lots of the same class and size added in the training window instead of build-out gives
@@ly_lo@@--@@ly_hi@@: it ranks districts well but underestimates how many homes are built.
\item \textbf{Manhattan rezonings} (Table~\ref{tab:replay}, Figure~\ref{fig:replay}). Capacity projected
@@g_capacity@@ homes on development sites, or @@g_prorated@@ pro-rated to 2025; @@g_observed@@ were built.
Probability $\times$ capacity gave @@g_likely@@, but the close total comes from errors that cancel: @@g_eh_ratio@@ and
@@g_in_ratio@@ times the observed in East Harlem and Inwood, and @@g_sn_ratio@@ times in SoHo/NoHo, where
@@g_soho_built@@ homes were built. Soft sites redeveloped in the training years added @@g_share@@ of their capacity
homes on average; scaling by that share gives @@g_yield@@ in total and a larger summed error across the three
(@@g_sae_yield@@ homes, against @@g_sae_lik@@), so the empirical yield did not improve these projections. The observed
count lies within the 95\% range of probability $\times$ capacity in @@of_g@@ rezonings; the ranges are conditional
on the model's probabilities and omit model error.
\end{itemize}

