\subsection{Optimizer and plausibility}\label{sec:r-opt}

\paragraph{Optimality} (algorithmic verification). In @@og_areas@@ small areas (one tax block each) every plan with up
to two changes was enumerated, and @@og_pos@@ had a plan better than no change. First-order search found the optimum
in @@og_first_frac@@ of these, and with exact re-scoring in @@og_polished_frac@@ (median @@og_sec@@ s). At district
scale no method guarantees an optimum, so methods are compared with the best plan any of them found
(Table~\ref{tab:opt}): exact greedy search found it in @@of_greedy@@ districts but took a median @@ob_greedy_sec@@ s;
first-order search with re-scoring reached a median @@ob_polish_share@@ of it in @@ob_polish_sec@@ s, the speed used in
the Studio.

\paragraph{Trade-off front.} On @@pt_med_n@@ tax blocks with @@pt_med_plans@@ possible plans each, the NSGA-III
explorer's front covered a median @@pt_med_hv@@ (minimum @@pt_med_hv_min@@) of the hypervolume of the exhaustive
front, against @@pt_rand_hv@@ for random search with as many evaluations; on @@pt_small_n@@ smaller blocks scored
exactly, it recovered the exact front. The test exposed terms missing from the first-order model, since added
(Supplement S6).

\paragraph{Plausibility} (Table~\ref{tab:realism}). Plans were recommended on the 2020 snapshot and compared with 2025,
with the same number of recommended parcels in each setting and one objective.
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Precedent.} Without realism layers, @@ra_noprec@@ of recommended changes had no precedent.
\item \textbf{Redevelopment.} Recommended parcels were redeveloped by 2025 at @@ra_eq_none@@ without realism layers,
@@ra_eq_prec@@ with the precedent filter and @@ra_eq_lik@@ with the likelihood layer, against @@ra_all@@ of all lots
and @@ra_fifth@@ of the likeliest fifth.
\item \textbf{Redevelopment is not implementation.} @@ra_into_phrase@@ recommended parcels became the recommended use.
\item \textbf{The cost.} The objective fell by @@ra_cost_prec@@ with the precedent filter and @@ra_cost_lik@@ with the
likelihood layer: the filter removes implausible conversions at little cost, while restricting candidates to likely
sites trades much of the objective for plausibility.
\end{itemize}

